<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toroodbe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Torodbe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toroodbe were a class of Muslim clerics and scholars among the Fulbe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, originally in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a>. From the late 17th century they led a series of Islamic reform movements, founding states such as Bundu, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">Imamate</a> of Futa Toro in 1776 and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, who came from a Toroodbe family, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. They defined themselves by learning rather than by descent.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Torodbe came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a>, an agricultural strip along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal-river">Senegal River</a> and once part of the state of Takrur, and spread from there among the Fulbe. They may have been a distinct group by the fifteenth century, when the Denianke conquered Takrur. In 1644 the Zawaya Berber reformer Nasr ad-Din launched a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> to restore purity of observance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> valley and gained the support of the Torodbe clerical clan against the region's traditional leaders. He was killed in 1674 and the movement was defeated by 1677, after which some Torodbe migrated south to Bundu and the Fouta Djallon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Torodbe first recruited from all levels of society, especially the poor, and included people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fula</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a>, Mande, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hausa">Hausa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> origin, but they spoke Fula, married into Fulbe families and became the Fulbe scholarly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>. Warrior groups looked down on them. They lived at first on charity, and the name is derived from tooraade, to ask for alms. They kept schools teaching in Arabic and built mosques that served as administrative centres, and they levied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tithe">tithes</a> on harvests and inheritances. Some made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and stayed for years in countries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>They aimed to purify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and to make Islamic law the law of the land. In practice, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futa-toro">Futa Toro</a> and Bundu, they became a closed society of a few families, and the divisions between slaves and free people and between hereditary occupations did not change.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Malick Sy founded the state of Bundu in the 1690s. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> began in Futa Jallon around 1726 or 1727, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karamokho-alfa">Karamokho Alfa</a>, who took the title almami. In Futa Toro Sulayman Baal led a jihad between 1769 and 1776, and in 1776 the Torodbe threw out the Denianke dynasty. His successor Abdul Kader became first Almamy of the theocratic Almamyate of Futa Toro. By his death in 1806 a few elite Torodbe families dominated the state.</p><p>In the nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, born into a Torodbe clan in 1754, led the largest of the Fulani jihads and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> in 1808. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-hadj-umar-tall">El Hadj Umar Tall</a> created the Toucouleur Empire. By the mid-nineteenth century the Torodbe almamis in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> had become hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchies</a>, and the French gave them political and economic support in return for permission to build fortified posts along the Senegal valley.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Torodbe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their Islam was considered lax by the Muslim scholars of Futa Toro, the <b>Torodbe</b>, who resented heavy taxes and the rulers' failure to protect villagers from enslavement.</blockquote><a href="/state/denianke-kingdom">Denianke Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Futa Toro region along the middle Senegal, <b>Torodbe</b> clerics rose in the 1760s and 1770s against the Denianke Kingdom and against raids by Moors from the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/imamate-of-futa-toro">Imamate of Futa Toro</a></li><li><blockquote>Bundu was founded around 1690 by Malik Sy, a <b>Torodbe</b> cleric from Futa Toro, who obtained land from the ruler of the Kingdom of Gajaaga and drew settlers to his community.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bundu">Kingdom of Bundu</a></li></ul></section>
